    add: [
      `## A realistic way to budget for a golf cart`,
      `Quoted prices can look very different from the final bill, so build your budget in layers. Start with the vehicle price, then add freight to your postcode, any options you want, and a plan for charging and storage. Then add running costs for the first few years: tyres, brakes, servicing and, for lead-acid carts, battery replacement. When you compare two carts, compare them on the same layers. A cheaper cart with a short warranty and a battery nearing the end of its life may cost more over three years than a dearer cart with lithium power and local support.`,
      `## Price drivers explained`,
      `### Number of seats`,
      `Two-seaters are the entry point and suit couples, golf and light property use. Four-seaters, often called 2+2 carts, add a rear-facing or forward-facing second row. Six and eight-seaters are built for resorts, schools and events, with heavier frames and stronger drive systems. Each step up in seating adds materials, motor load and battery demand, which is reflected in the price.`,
      `### Battery chemistry and capacity`,
      `The battery is the single most important component in an electric cart. Lithium packs cost more up front, but they are lighter, charge faster and need no watering. Lead-acid sets cost less at purchase but are heavy and eventually need replacing. If a quote does not say what battery is fitted, ask. The ampere-hour or kilowatt-hour rating tells you how far the cart can go, so compare it with how far you drive on a typical day.`,
      `### Build quality and brand`,
      `A well-supported brand has parts, manuals and service knowledge available in Australia. That matters when a controller, charger or tyre needs replacing. Ask who services the cart, how long parts take to arrive and what the warranty covers. A strong warranty on the battery and motor is a good sign.`,
      `### Options and accessories`,
      `Roofs, windscreens, lighting kits, mirrors, bag racks, seat covers, floor mats and wheel upgrades all add to the total. Some are worth buying with the cart, especially lights, a windscreen and seat belts. Others can wait. Our [golf cart accessories](/parts-accessories/golf-cart-accessories/) range lets you add items later.`,
      `## Typical ownership costs`,
      `- **Electricity:** charging an electric cart costs little compared with fuel, but it depends on your tariff and how often you drive.`,
      `- **Tyres and wheels:** wear depends on surface and load. Upgrades such as mag wheels are cosmetic but popular.`,
      `- **Brakes and suspension:** inspect regularly, particularly on hilly properties.`,
      `- **Servicing:** annual checks keep warranties valid and catch problems early.`,
      `- **Storage and security:** a dry, ventilated space and a lockable shed protect your investment.`,
      `## Petrol, electric and lithium compared`,
      `Petrol carts, such as our [petrol golf carts](/golf-carts/petrol/), suit long distances and heavy use because refuelling is quick, but they are louder and need regular engine servicing. Electric carts are quiet, simple and cheap to run. Lithium electric carts add lighter weight and quick charging. For a full comparison of the battery options read our guide to [lithium vs lead-acid golf cart batteries](/blog/lithium-vs-lead-acid-golf-cart-batteries/).`,
      `## Where to save and where not to`,
      `Save on cosmetic options, extra accessories and premium trim. Do not save on the battery, brakes, seat belts and warranty. If you are tempted by a very low price, check what is missing: the charger, the roof, the seat belts or the warranty. Compare delivered prices, not sticker prices, and ask for the specification in writing.`,
      `## How to compare two quotes`,
      `Put both quotes side by side and tick off the same items: seats, battery type and capacity, charger, roof and windscreen, lights, tyres, freight and warranty. If one quote is missing an item, ask for it to be added so the comparison is fair. Ask about delivery time and what happens if the cart arrives damaged. A clear, itemised quote is a good sign of a seller who stands behind the product.`,
      `## Finance, payment and GST`,
      `All of our prices include 10% GST, and delivery is free over $1,000, with a flat $95 below that. The minimum order is $250. You can pay by bank transfer (Osko or EFT) or PayID, or use Bitcoin or USDT for an extra 10% off, applied automatically at checkout. If you are buying for a business, ask your accountant about how GST and depreciation apply to your situation.`,
    ],
